  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef is interested in the sum of GCDs of all prefixes of a permutation of the integers {1,2,…,N}\\{1, 2, \\ldots, N\\}{1,2,…,N}.\n\nFormally, for a permutation P=[P1,P2,…,PN]P = [P_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_N]P=[P1​,P2​,…,PN​] of {1,2,…,N}\\{1, 2, \\ldots, N\\}{1,2,…,N}, let us define a function Fi=gcd⁡(A1,A2,A3,…,Ai)F_i = \\gcd(A_1, A_2, A_3, \\ldots, A_i)Fi​=gcd(A1​,A2​,A3​,…,Ai​). Chef is interested in the value of F1+F2+…+FNF_1 + F_2 + \\ldots + F_NF1​+F2​+…+FN​.\n\nNow, Chef wants to find a permutation of {1,2,…,N}\\{1, 2, \\ldots, N\\}{1,2,…,N} which has the given sum equal to XXX. Please help Chef find one such permutation. In case there is no such permutation, print −1-1−1. In case of multiple answers, any of them will be accepted.\n\nA permutation of {1,2,…,N}\\{1, 2, \\ldots, N\\}{1,2,…,N} is a sequence of numbers from 111 to NNN in which each number occurs exactly once.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of a single line containing two space separated integers NNN and XXX denoting the length of required permutation and the required sum of GCDs of all prefixes respectively.\n\n\nOutput Format\n\nIf there is a valid permutation that satisfies the required condition, then:\n\nIn a single line, output NNN space-separated integers denoting the required permutation permutation.\n\n\nIf there is no permutation, print −1-1−1 in a single line.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\leq T \\leq 10^41≤T≤104",
      "1≤N≤10001 \\leq N \\leq 10001≤N≤1000",
      "1≤X≤2⋅N−11 \\leq X \\leq 2\\cdot N - 11≤X≤2⋅N−1",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 3⋅1053\\cdot 10^53⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n1 1\n2 1\n4 6\n3 5",
        "output": "1\n-1\n2 4 3 1\n3 2 1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The only possible permutation has a sum of 111, as required.\nTest case 222: The two permutations of length 222 are:\n\n[1,2][1, 2][1,2] with a value of 1+1=21 + 1 = 21+1=2\n[2,1][2, 1][2,1] with a value of 2+1=32 + 1 = 32+1=3\n\nNone of them have a sum of X=1X = 1X=1, so we output −1-1−1.\nTest case 333: For P=[2,4,3,1]P = [2, 4, 3, 1]P=[2,4,3,1], we have:\n\nF1=2F_1 = 2F1​=2\nF2=gcd⁡(2,4)=2F_2 = \\gcd(2, 4) = 2F2​=gcd(2,4)=2\nF3=gcd⁡(2,4,3)=1F_3 = \\gcd(2, 4, 3) = 1F3​=gcd(2,4,3)=1\nF4=gcd⁡(2,4,3,1)=1F_4 = \\gcd(2, 4, 3, 1) = 1F4​=gcd(2,4,3,1)=1\n\nThe sum of these values is 666, as required."
      }
    ]
  },
